1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,921
Welcome to Windward, a pilot designed to
make tomorrow's wind outlook clearer before the buying

2
00:00:05,921 --> 00:00:11,945
decision. The promise is straightforward: see the
forecast that was actually available at your chosen

3
00:00:11,945 --> 00:00:19,034
cutoff, understand its limitations, and review its
subsequent accuracy. One important disclosure: this walkthrough uses

4
00:00:19,034 --> 00:00:25,295
clearly labelled synthetic test data because the
authoring environment could not reach Elexon's data API.

5
00:00:25,295 --> 00:00:31,792
It demonstrates working software and analysis logic,
not measured January market results. This opening screen

6
00:00:31,792 --> 00:00:33,919
refuses to invent a real data feed.

7
00:00:34,869 --> 00:00:39,725
The decision desk starts with the date
on which the team is buying. January decision

8
00:00:39,725 --> 00:00:44,696
dates cover delivery from the second of
January through the first of February. Set the

9
00:00:44,696 --> 00:00:50,581
local decision cutoff and, optionally, an availability
buffer. The curve then uses the latest eligible

10
00:00:50,581 --> 00:00:55,838
publication for each delivery half hour. It
does not use a better forecast published later.

11
00:00:55,838 --> 00:01:01,666
The headline cards show forecast power, covered
energy, coverage and age, so a complete-looking curve

12
00:01:01,666 --> 00:01:03,951
cannot conceal stale or missing inputs.

13
00:01:04,901 --> 00:01:10,360
Below the curve, the interval table makes
the result inspectable. Each row shows the delivery

14
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:16,516
time, selected forecast, publication timestamp, age at
the decision and change from its previous eligible

15
00:01:16,516 --> 00:01:21,568
version. The export carries those same fields
and a snapshot hash, giving an analyst a

16
00:01:21,568 --> 00:01:27,027
reproducible trail from the screen to the
underlying data. Different half hours may come from

17
00:01:27,027 --> 00:01:32,834
different publication vintages. We make that visible
rather than implying that the whole curve came

18
00:01:32,834 --> 00:01:34,693
from one complete forecast run.

19
00:01:35,643 --> 00:01:41,411
Turning on actuals deliberately changes the task
from decision support to hindsight review. The actual

20
00:01:41,411 --> 00:01:46,115
curve makes misses easy to see, while
the warning reminds us that those values were

21
00:01:46,115 --> 00:01:51,212
not available when the team bought power.
Forecast minus actual is the error convention: a

22
00:01:51,212 --> 00:01:57,764
positive value means wind underdelivered against the
forecast. That direction matters. A consistent overforecast can

23
00:01:57,764 --> 00:02:03,140
create a different operational concern from errors
of the same average size in both directions.

24
00:02:04,090 --> 00:02:10,225
The accuracy lab answers four complementary questions.
Mean absolute error shows typical miss size. Root

25
00:02:10,225 --> 00:02:16,594
mean squared error gives larger misses more
weight. Bias shows direction. Weighted absolute percentage error

26
00:02:16,594 --> 00:02:22,729
adds a relative measure without dividing each
interval by near-zero wind. Coverage remains explicit. The

27
00:02:22,729 --> 00:02:29,331
daily bars, hour-of-day profile and lead-time breakdown
help locate where performance changes. Selecting a daily

28
00:02:29,331 --> 00:02:35,232
bar returns to its decision snapshot. These
displayed numbers illustrate the workflow only; they are

29
00:02:35,232 --> 00:02:36,926
not January Elexon findings.

30
00:02:37,876 --> 00:02:44,111
This deliberately incomplete test day demonstrates a
crucial behaviour: missing forecasts stay as gaps, and

31
00:02:44,111 --> 00:02:49,538
energy is labelled as a covered-interval total.
The optional amber range also has an explicit

32
00:02:49,538 --> 00:02:55,658
boundary. It uses only earlier January decisions
and actual publications already available at the current

33
00:02:55,658 --> 00:03:01,662
cutoff. Early January may therefore have insufficient
history. Once enough history exists, the range is

34
00:03:01,662 --> 00:03:07,493
still descriptive, not a guaranteed probability band
or a recommendation to buy a particular volume.

35
00:03:08,443 --> 00:03:15,802
The methodology page puts assumptions next to
the analysis: source datasets, timestamp selection, metric definitions,

36
00:03:15,802 --> 00:03:22,039
quality counts and provenance. The product uses
only the specified wind forecast and actual series.

37
00:03:22,039 --> 00:03:27,777
National wind is not the utility's contracted
wind entitlement, and the app has no customer

38
00:03:27,777 --> 00:03:34,076
demand, existing hedges or prices. It therefore
does not claim a purchase quantity, thermal dispatch

39
00:03:34,076 --> 00:03:39,439
requirement or cost saving. The value of
this pilot is a clearer, auditable basis for

40
00:03:39,439 --> 00:03:41,747
the team's forecast-risk discussion.

41
00:03:42,697 --> 00:03:48,550
For pilot acceptance, the next gate is
evidence rather than another dashboard feature: import the

42
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:55,000
real Elexon history, reconcile sample selections and
errors, confirm the desk cutoff, and validate coverage

43
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:01,211
with the team. Then publish the accepted
application and replace this illustrative results segment with

44
00:04:01,211 --> 00:04:08,796
real findings. The implementation already separates a
timely forecast view from retrospective evaluation, exposes uncertainty,

45
00:04:08,796 --> 00:04:13,633
and preserves an audit trail. That is
the customer workflow. I will now show the

46
00:04:13,633 --> 00:04:15,425
engineering design behind it.

47
00:04:16,375 --> 00:04:22,739
For the technical review, the architecture is
intentionally small. React provides the three product views.

48
00:04:22,739 --> 00:04:28,449
FastAPI exposes read-only analysis and CSV routes
and serves the frontend from the same origin.

49
00:04:28,449 --> 00:04:34,456
A separate Python importer builds an immutable
historical snapshot. A fixed one-month pilot does not

50
00:04:34,456 --> 00:04:40,403
need a database, message broker or request-time
data fetching. The main design priority is temporal

51
00:04:40,403 --> 00:04:45,756
correctness: the definition of what was known
must be enforced in the backend, not merely

52
00:04:45,756 --> 00:04:47,659
described by a dashboard label.

53
00:04:48,609 --> 00:04:56,032
The importer fetches publication-day windows, retains raw
responses with request metadata and hashes, validates records,

54
00:04:56,032 --> 00:05:02,105
and atomically replaces the accepted snapshot. It
retries transient failures but does not treat an

55
00:05:02,105 --> 00:05:10,263
error response as an empty successful dataset.
Exact duplicates collapse; conflicting target-and-publication values are quarantined.

56
00:05:10,263 --> 00:05:15,907
The repository layer verifies source mode and
adds the snapshot hash. The live API contract

57
00:05:15,907 --> 00:05:22,225
still needs a successful network validation. The
current importer tests exercise mock responses, not a

58
00:05:22,225 --> 00:05:24,065
hidden real January download.

59
00:05:25,015 --> 00:05:30,215
These are actual source excerpts from the
domain module. For each target, the forecast list

60
00:05:30,215 --> 00:05:35,811
is filtered by the buffered decision cutoff
before selecting its last eligible version. The actual

61
00:05:35,811 --> 00:05:41,577
lookup has a separate availability guard: the
interval must be complete, and the actual's publication

62
00:05:41,577 --> 00:05:46,721
must be no later than the permitted
time. This matters because filtering only the delivery

63
00:05:46,721 --> 00:05:52,713
date would still leak later corrections. Retrospective
scoring deliberately uses the latest actual in the

64
00:05:52,713 --> 00:05:56,330
fetched window and states that this is not settlement finality.

65
00:05:57,280 --> 00:06:03,586
Metrics operate on exact matched timestamps. Missing
values remain null, zero remains valid, and aggregate

66
00:06:03,586 --> 00:06:10,303
scores use all matched intervals rather than
averaging rounded daily summaries. Energy multiplies half-hour power

67
00:06:10,303 --> 00:06:15,607
by half an hour. The prior-only range
uses earlier January decisions, with both a minimum

68
00:06:15,607 --> 00:06:22,501
number of days and intervals. These thresholds
are transparent heuristics, not statistical guarantees. Adjacent half

69
00:06:22,501 --> 00:06:29,396
hours are correlated, and no out-of-sample interval
calibration or monetary optimisation is claimed. The methodology

70
00:06:29,396 --> 00:06:31,989
document makes those boundaries reviewable.

71
00:06:32,939 --> 00:06:38,861
The frontend uses React hooks, native JavaScript
modules and SVG charts. It does not reimplement

72
00:06:38,861 --> 00:06:45,150
forecast selection. Request identity checks prevent an
old response from rendering in a newly selected

73
00:06:45,150 --> 00:06:50,645
tab, and chart paths break across missing
intervals. Exact data is available in the table

74
00:06:50,645 --> 00:06:57,117
and CSV. One dependency caveat is explicit:
offline authoring required a locally available React runtime,

75
00:06:57,117 --> 00:07:03,649
whose ReactDOM build has a prerelease identifier.
Replace it with matching audited maintained packages and

76
00:07:03,649 --> 00:07:06,946
rerun the browser suites before a production release.

77
00:07:07,896 --> 00:07:15,410
The executed checks include fifty-three Python tests,
six JavaScript tests and thirteen Chromium assertions. Python

78
00:07:15,410 --> 00:07:23,377
statement coverage rounds to ninety-four percent. Adversarial
tests cover late revisions, exact cutoff boundaries, missing

79
00:07:23,377 --> 00:07:31,409
versus zero values, corrupted caches, incomplete coverage
and the January-to-February boundary. Browser checks exercise the

80
00:07:31,409 --> 00:07:38,210
actual React components against FastAPI through a
local test bridge. Because this sandbox blocks browser

81
00:07:38,210 --> 00:07:44,364
HTTP navigation, that is not a full
HTTP or security-policy test. Separate HTTP, container and

82
00:07:44,364 --> 00:07:47,862
hosted checks are supplied but not claimed as passed.

83
00:07:48,812 --> 00:07:55,581
The handover includes a non-root Dockerfile, read-only
Compose data mount, Vercel FastAPI configuration and a

84
00:07:55,581 --> 00:08:02,781
prepared continuous-integration workflow. The app reads a
fixed snapshot; replacing that snapshot requires a restart

85
00:08:02,781 --> 00:08:08,997
or redeployment. Docker and Vercel are separate
delivery paths, and neither is described as executed

86
00:08:08,997 --> 00:08:15,397
here. The repository guide maps each file,
gives startup and testing commands, discloses AI assistance,

87
00:08:15,397 --> 00:08:21,305
and lists known limitations. A local API
explorer and this technical review page make knowledge

88
00:08:21,305 --> 00:08:24,628
transfer easier without external script dependencies.

89
00:08:25,578 --> 00:08:31,474
The remaining release gates are explicit: accept
real Elexon data, audit the runtime, execute the

90
00:08:31,474 --> 00:08:37,551
container and HTTP checks, create the anonymous
public GitHub repository, deploy the app, and upload

91
00:08:37,551 --> 00:08:42,906
the reviewed video as unlisted. Open all
three submitted links in a fresh private window

92
00:08:42,906 --> 00:08:48,982
and play the complete video before submitting
the Microsoft form. Those account-based steps have not

93
00:08:48,982 --> 00:08:55,962
occurred in this environment. The supplied code,
tests, documentation and walkthrough provide the handover, without

94
00:08:55,962 --> 00:09:00,474
inventing successful publishing actions
or real-market results. Thank you.
